Q: Is there any wat to fit the content into single page in Select PDF .Net Core? A: <p>Late to this one, but leaving it here in case someone hits the same thing in the future.</p>
<p>The <code>AutoFit*</code> options only <em>scale</em> the content to the page width — they won't force everything onto one page. For &quot;keep all the content on a single page, however tall it needs to be,&quot; SelectPdf has a dedicated option for exactly this: <strong><code>GenerateSinglePagePdf</code></strong>. It resizes the PDF page so the whole rendered document fits on one page.</p>
<p>Also, there's a subtle bug in your snippet: you set <code>PdfPageCustomSize</code> and then immediately set <code>PdfPageSize = A4</code>, so the custom size is thrown away (A4 wins). In single-page mode you don't need the custom size at all — the converter works out the page height for you:</p>
<pre><code>var pdf = new SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf();

// fit everything onto one page (the page height grows to fit the content)
pdf.Options.GenerateSinglePagePdf = true;

// optional: the virtual browser width used to render the HTML
pdf.Options.WebPageWidth = 1024;

// sets the page *width*; height is handled automatically in single-page mode
pdf.Options.PdfPageSize = SelectPdf.PdfPageSize.A4;

var pdfDoc = pdf.ConvertHtmlString(html);
using var ms = new MemoryStream();
pdfDoc.Save(ms);
pdfDoc.Close();   // release the converter resources
return ms.ToArray();
</code></pre>
<p>One caveat: single-page output produces one very tall page, and the PDF format caps page dimensions at 14,400 pt (200 in). So for <em>extremely</em> long HTML a single page may not be physically possible — in that case you'd fall back to letting it paginate, or scale the content down to a fixed page instead.</p>
<p><em>Disclosure: I work on SelectPdf.</em></p>
